  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nWeak low level ascent in warm advection/weak 925 mb warm front \nshould result in a potential for isolated areas of very light \nrain early this morning. This should lift north of the area\nlater this morning, as a ridge begins to build in across the\nUpper Ohio Valley region head of a deepening trough across the\ncentral CONUS. This ridge will maintain dry weather and warm\ntemperatures into early Wednesday, before shifting east. High\ntemperatures, both today and Wednesday, are expected to be 15 to\n20 degrees above average for this time of the year. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe main upper trough is expected to deepen into a closed low\nacross the Upper Midwest on Wednesday. An initial shortwave in\nsouthwest flow ahead of the low, along with a surface occluded\nfront, should bring increasing rain chances by afternoon into\nthe evening as this approach and cross the Upper Ohio Valley\nregion. Some jet assisted ascent aloft should also bring some\nenhancement to the coverage of the rain by Wednesday night.\nThe most likely QPF amounts range from 0.25 to 0.5 inches. Lower\nend (10th percentile) amounts are generally 0.1 to 0.25 inches,\nwith high end amounts (90th percentile) ranging from 0.7 to 1.3\ninches, with the highest amounts across Ohio. \n\nThe initial shortwave and surface occlusion should exit to the\neast of the area Thursday morning, with decreasing rain chances.\nModel ensembles then indicate a more active, amplified pattern\nthrough early next week, with a broad upper trough across the NE\nCONUS. A series of embedded shortwave troughs should then\nperiodically cross the area, keeping occasional rain chances in\nthe forecast. With decreasing 500 mb heights, temperatures\nshould gradually fall to more seasonably cool readings over the\nweekend.",
